  "command": "bash scripts/run-mutual-checked-record-native-ci.sh",
  "scope": "The native installed lane authenticates the ordinary mutual recursive checked-record selection for c, cpp. Original inputs, exact failures, preserved rejected values, recovery and proof-free results are retained; independently reproduced packages are installed without author sources and replayed after relocation where the host permits it. Hosted Ubuntu 24.04 Linux x64 acceptance records the observed glibc runtime and enforces a glibc 2.38 package floor; other operating systems and architectures remain separate requirements. Ordinary source and independently reviewed Binding IR routes reproduce complete C and C++ packages from two isolated author roots, remove author sources, install offline, relocate and replay without consumer sources or compilers. The immutable C and C++ callers cover mutual records and variants, aliases, arrays and options, active branches, exact Fin and Subtype diagnostics, malformed values, cycles, depth limits, preserved rejected inputs and recovery. Proof fields are erased, recursive nominal identities stay distinct, and results contain no runtime proof constructors. Callbacks, owned values, asynchronous calls, retained identity, dependent index kinds, open dependent records and zero-payload records remain separate requirements. Original package archives, receipts, models, source inputs, caller sources, reports, controls, TAP, checker output, toolchain log and exact producer, checker and archiver source closures are retained; external compiler and operating-system distributions remain trusted. Recursive checked-record acceptance covers the tested Packet self-cycle and Envelope ↔ Step mutual cycle, their aliases, Array/Option wrappers, active branches, Nat Subtype constructors and Fin 3 fields in copied parameters, results and fields. It does not establish generic recursive records, other recursive graph shapes, value-carrying receiver-dependent fields, other index kinds, open dependent or zero-payload records, callbacks or owned positions.",
