  "scope": "The jvm installed lane authenticates the ordinary mutual recursive checked-record selection for java, kotlin. Original inputs, exact failures, preserved rejected values, recovery and proof-free results are retained; independently reproduced packages are installed without author sources and replayed after relocation where the host permits it. The archive authenticates local Linux x64/glibc 2.36 acceptance; it does not claim hosted-runner acceptance or promote support before the whole recursive-record matrix closes. Both ordinary source and independently reviewed Binding IR routes reproduce complete Maven packages from two isolated author roots, remove author sources, install offline, relocate, and replay without consumer sources. Each route runs the unchanged typed Java caller and route-bound Kotlin caller three times. Java records 590 checks, 72 native rejections, five host rejections, 290 calls and snapshots, 77 recoveries and 64 rounds per execution; Kotlin records 593 checks, 72 native rejections, six host rejections, 292 calls and snapshots, 78 recoveries and 64 rounds. The fixture covers mutually recursive records and variants, transparent aliases, active Option and variant branches, exact Subtype and Fin failures, null carriers, cycles, depth limits, immutable callers, and constructor-free results. Kotlin additionally proves three compile-time non-null rejections through positive and negative compiler controls; Java proves its record carriers refuse cycle construction before runtime traversal. The original JARs and POMs, receipts, generated-source identities, nullability diagnostics, execution records, and selected producer/checker sources are retained. The complete JDK and Kotlin distribution remain trusted external tools represented by recorded identities. Recursive checked-record acceptance covers the tested Packet self-cycle and Envelope ↔ Step mutual cycle, their aliases, Array/Option wrappers, active branches, Nat Subtype constructors and Fin